{"timestamp": "2026-02-04 17:24:02.122323", "event": "llm_start", "messages": "content=\"You are provided with the following network description and its current state:\\nNetwork Description: This experiment uses a scalable SDN spine–leaf topology whose size depends on the selected topo_size.\\nEach leaf switch connects to all spine switches using point-to-point links.\\nEach leaf switch connects to two hosts belong to the same subnet 10.0.0.0/24.\\nAll switches also join a management network 20.0.0.0/24.\\nThe SDN controller resides at 20.0.0.100 and manages all switches via OpenFlow.\\nOVS switches: leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1\\nHosts: host_1_1, host_1_2, host_2_1, host_2_2\\nTopology: (leaf_1:eth0, host_1_1:eth0), (leaf_1:eth1, host_1_2:eth0), (leaf_2:eth0, host_2_1:eth0), (leaf_2:eth1, host_2_2:eth0), (spine_1:eth0, leaf_1:eth2), (spine_1:eth1, leaf_2:eth2), (spine_1:eth2, leaf_1:eth3)\\n\\nYour goal is to analyze the network condition and, if needed, use the available tools.\\nYou need to generate a troubeshooting diagnosis report.\\nThe report should reflect your assessment of the network's health, indicate any abnormal behavior you identify, and describe relevant findings based on your analysis.\\n\\nFocus on producing an informative and coherent diagnostic report derived from the network state.\\nDo not need to propose any solutions or remediation steps at this stage.\" additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={} id='35def876-00ff-4fc6-be76-2af91c3920e8'", "model": {"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "chat_models", "openai", "ChatOpenAI"], "kwargs": {"model_name": "kt-gpt-oss-rh014", "temperature": 0.0, "openai_api_key": {"lc": 1, "type": "secret", "id": ["OPENAI_API_KEY"]}, "openai_api_base": "https://dev.kora.kt.co.kr/v2", "request_timeout": 180.0, "max_retries": 2}, "name": "ChatOpenAI"}}
{"timestamp": "2026-02-04 17:24:13.974960", "event": "llm_end", "text": "**Network Troubleshooting Diagnosis – Preliminary Report**  \n*Date: 2026‑02‑04*  \n\n---\n\n### 1. Scope & Objectives\n| Item | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **Network** | SDN spine‑leaf topology (leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1) with four hosts (host_1_1, host_1_2, host_2_1, host_2_2). All devices are OVS instances managed by an OpenFlow controller at **20.0.0.100**. |\n| **Goal** | Detect any anomaly, localize the faulty device(s), and identify the root cause. No remediation is to be suggested at this stage. |\n| **Methodology** | – Gather runtime information (link status, OpenFlow flow tables, controller‑switch connectivity, host reachability). <br>– Correlate observations with the intended design. |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Current Information Available\n| Source | Data Provided |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **Network description** | Topology, IP sub‑netting, management network, and physical links are known. |\n| **Runtime state** | *No live telemetry, ping results, flow tables, or controller logs have been supplied.* |\n\n> **Result:** With only the static topology description, we cannot confirm whether the network is operating as intended. The absence of live data prevents us from verifying link health, OpenFlow rule installation, or end‑to‑end connectivity.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Diagnostic Gaps & Required Evidence  \n\n| Diagnostic Area | Specific Checks Needed | Reason for Check |\n|-----------------|------------------------|------------------|\n| **Controller‑to‑Switch channel** | - `ovs-vsctl show` on each switch (leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1). <br>- OpenFlow connection state (`ovs-appctl bridge/dump-flows` or `ovs-ofctl dump-ports`). | To confirm that each OVS instance is successfully connected to the controller (20.0.0.100) and that the controller is alive. |\n| **Physical/link layer status** | - `ovs-ofctl show <bridge>` to list ports and their state (up/down). <br>- `ethtool` or `ip link` output for each interface (eth0‑eth3). | Detect any down or error‑state links (e.g., leaf‑spine or leaf‑host). |\n| **OpenFlow flow tables** | - `ovs-ofctl dump-flows <bridge>` for leaf_1, leaf_2, spine_1. | Verify that the controller has programmed the expected forwarding rules (e.g., host‑to‑host traffic across the spine). |\n| **Host reachability** | - ICMP ping from each host to the other three hosts (e.g., `ping 10.0.0.2`). <br>- Ping from each host to the controller (20.0.0.100). | Confirm end‑to‑end connectivity and that the management network is functional. |\n| **Controller health** | - Controller process status (e.g., `systemctl status ryu` or equivalent). <br>- Controller logs for OpenFlow handshake errors. | Ensure the SDN control plane is operational. |\n| **ARP/Neighbour tables** | - `arp -n` on each host and on each switch (via `ovs-vsctl`). | Detect missing ARP entries that could indicate a broken data‑plane path. |\n| **Packet captures (optional)** | - `tcpdump -i <iface>` on a leaf‑spine link while generating traffic. | Provide low‑level evidence of dropped or malformed frames if higher‑level checks are inconclusive. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Preliminary Assessment (Based on Available Data)\n\n| Observation | Interpretation |\n|-------------|----------------|\n| **Topology is correctly defined** (leaf‑spine full mesh, each leaf attached to two hosts, management network present). | No design‑time issues identified. |\n| **No runtime metrics** (link status, flow tables, ping results) have been supplied. | Unable to confirm health; potential anomalies could exist in any of the following layers: <br>• Physical/link (cable, port) <br>• OpenFlow control (handshake, rule push) <br>• Data‑plane forwarding (missing/incorrect flows) <br>• Host configuration (IP, subnet mask) |\n| **Management IP range (20.0.0.0/24)** is distinct from host subnet (10.0.0.0/24). | This separation is expected; however, we need to verify that each device has a correctly assigned management IP and can reach the controller. |\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nAt this stage we cannot declare the network “healthy” nor can we pinpoint any fault, because the essential operational data is missing. The network **may be functioning**, but the lack of verification leaves a **potential anomaly** undetected.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Recommended Data‑Collection Actions (No remediation)\n\n1. **Execute the checks listed in Section 3** on each switch, the controller, and all hosts.  \n2. **Collect the output** of the following commands (or their equivalents) and provide them for analysis:  \n\n   - `ovs-vsctl show` (per switch)  \n   - `ovs-ofctl dump-flows <bridge>` (per switch)  \n   - `ovs-ofctl show <bridge>` (port status)  \n   - `ping -c 3 10.0.0.X` from each host to every other host and to 20.0.0.100  \n   - `arp -n` on hosts and switches  \n   - Controller logs (e.g., `/var/log/ryu/ryu.log` or equivalent)  \n\n3. **If any port reports “down” or any flow table is empty**, note those specific ports/devices for further localization.\n\nOnce the above telemetry is supplied, a definitive anomaly detection, fault localization, and root‑cause identification can be performed.  \n\n---  \n\n*Prepared by: Network Troubleshooting Analyst (SDN focus)*  \n*No remediation steps are included as per the current request.*", "generation_info": {"finish_reason": "stop", "logprobs": null}, "invalid_tool_calls": [], "usage_metadata": {"input_tokens": 499, "output_tokens": 1610, "total_tokens": 2109, "input_token_details": {}, "output_token_details": {}}}
